# MOFs-Based Heterogeneous Catalysts: New Opportunities for Energy-Related CO              <sub/>2</sub>              Conversion

## MOFs-Based Heterogeneous Catalysts: New Opportunities for Energy-Related $\mathbf{CO}_{2}$ Conversion
Metal–organic frameworks (MOFs) with high surface area and tunable chemical structures have attracted tremendous attention. Recently, the utilization of solar energy for $\mathsf{c o}_{2}$ conversion to produce valuable chemicals or fuels has become extremely appealing. The interior of MOFs can be designed to have defects, heteroatoms and embedded nanoscale metal catalysts for the development of $\mathsf{c o}_{2}$ conversion. In this review, the recent development of MOFs-based catalysts for $\mathsf{c o}_{2}$ conversion reactions, including photocatalysis and electrocatalysis are summarized. In particular, the preparation and mechanism of $\mathsf{c o}_{2}$ conversion-based MOFs are discussed. The examples are expected to provide deeper understanding in preparing highly active and stable MOFs-based $\mathsf{c o}_{2}$ conversion materials.
## 1. Introduction
Environmental and energy-related issues have become the major concerns to the public. Through the ages, the over development and overuse of carbonic fossil fuels, such as coal, petroleum, and natural gas, have provided nearly 200 years of energy supply. However, the overuse of these fossil fuels and the subsequent greenhouse effect/energy crisis has become one of the most important questions which the humanity must face.[1,2] Global observations evidenced a massive emission of the greenhouse gases [GHGs, $98\%$ of carbon dioxide $\left(\mathrm{CO}_{2}\right)$ , remaining small amount of methane $\mathrm{(CH_{4})}$ and nitrous oxide $\left(\mathrm{N}_{2}\mathrm{O}\right)]$ , mainly from the consumption of fossil fuels, caused climatic warming and glacial ablation worldwide. To mitigate the greenhouse effect, scientists call for a worldwide $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ reduction in the utilization of conventional fossil fuels and intensively seek for environmental-friendly alternatives;[3–6] on the other hand, establishment of carbon trading and technologies of carbon sequestration are forged ahead steadily.[7–11] Remarkably, researchers attempted various efforts to make $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ capture and fixation, involving biomass,[12,13] physical[14] and chemical approaches.[15] However, most of the current ways for alleviating $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ concentration are time-consuming and even easier to reach the capacity limitation. Not only that, adoption of some sequestration methods, like ocean dump, may give rise to another environmental disaster.[16–18]
Therefore, at existing unsatisfied performance level of $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ fixation, pushing forward the closed-circulation of atmospheric carbon along with promotion of clean-energy utilization can perform carbon neutral, and even reduction.[19] As one of the cheapest and abundant carbon sources in the nature, conversion of atmospheric $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ to carbonaceous energy resource carriers is an extremely attractive option, since the goals of $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ emission relief and alternative energy production can be both achieved.[20] However, the essential chemical inertness of $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ obstructs the processes of reactions beyond the goal accomplishment.[21] The thermodynamic and kinetic energy barriers need to be overcome to obtain complete chemical conversion. Moreover, chemically converting $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ to reduction products involves the participation of multiple electrons, and is more difficult and complex than another classic reaction of energy production, i.e., $\mathrm{H}_{2}$ generation from water splitting; since the carbonaceous products of the former are nonselective, and the reaction is more unfavorable.[22,23] Comparing to the researches on artificial $\mathrm{H}_{2}$ production, the knowledge referring to efficient and selective reduction of $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ to energy-rich molecules is urgent to be updated.
$\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ conversion is often traced back to 1972 that reported by Honda and Fujishima with using inorganic photocatalysts $\mathrm{TiO}_{2}$ .[24] As the capture of $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ has already been studied in metal–organic frameworks (MOFs),[25–28] it has recently been studied for their use as catalysts for the reduction of $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ into high-value chemicals.[14,29]
MOFs hold great promise for applications in the field of $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ reduction, which can act directly as the catalysts or as components to promote $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ reduction in a hybrid catalytic system. The interior of MOFs can be designed to have open metal sites, specific heteroatoms, functionalized organic ligand, other building unit interactions, hydrophobicity, defects, porosity, and embedded nanoscale metal catalysts which is crucial for the development of better $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ reduction performance MOFs.[30–32] Due to the poor electron conductivity of MOFs,[33,34] and the inaccessibility of all the catalytic active sites to the reactants, the stability of MOFs in water and under UV light irradiation need to be further improved. We believe that a comprehensive and up-to-date review summarizing the recent applications of MOFs for $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ reduction will contribute to improve theoretical understanding of this field. In order to make a better achievement, it is necessary to use the MOF to build more complex materials to address $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ capacity and reduction ability together in one material. Future MOFs materials for $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ reduction should be economical and environmental friendly. The keys for promoting catalysis include structural defects in the MOFs, open metal sites from metal clusters, Lewis acid sites from the metal cluster and organometallic linker, and cocatalyst functionalized MOFs.
Several reviews focusing on MOFs for $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ conversion have been recently reviewed and discussed. Dhakshinamoorthy et al. pointed out that photoexcitation of the light absorbing units in MOFs often generates a ligand-to-metal charge-separation state that can result in photocatalytic activity. Maina et al. discussed the corelationship between the properties of MOF materials including their $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ reduction catalytic performance under different reaction conditions. Li and co-workers have summarized the recent applications of MOFs for photocatalytic $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ reduction, in which MOFs can act as the photocatalysts for $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ reduction or as components in a hybrid photocatalytic system to promote $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ reduction. Yaghi and co-workers also pointed out that the challenge in developing catalysts for $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ photo- or electroreduction is rooted in the interplay between selectivity, activity, and efficiency.[35–38]
## 2. Synthetic Approaches for MOFs
MOFs, also called porous coordination polymers, are extended framework with open structures constructed from metal ions and organic ligands linked via coordination bonds and other weak chemical bonds.[39] Due to their rich physical/chemical properties, the area of MOFs has recently experienced tremendous explosive growth in research activity.[40–43] They provide numerous advantages including ultrahigh porosity, large surface area, tunable topology, and flexible chemical functionalities.[44–48]
