MOF-based materials, including pristine MOFs,[49,50] MOF composites (e.g., $\mathrm{C_{3}N_{4}/M O F}$ composite, metal nanoparticles $@$ MOF, and $\mathrm{TiO}_{2}/\mathrm{MOF}$ composite),[51–55] and MOF-derived materials[56,57] are well studied for the catalysis purposes. For most MOFs are insulator, design MOF-based materials as efficient catalysts for $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ reduction, their charge mobility, and electronic conductivity should be carefully considered. Some MOFs related materials to $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ reduction have been listed in Table 1. The catalytic activity and selectivity are highly dependent on the involved metal element. Metal centers are usually considered as catalytic active sites caused by the diversification of its valence state.[44] Additionally, some metal clusters, especially Fe–O clusters, have been manifested that they possess the visible-light response and can be excited and generate charge carriers.[58–60] Some organic ligands can serve as antennas to utilize visible light inherently, excitons will be produced upon the illumination and transfer to the metal sites based on the linker-tometal-cluster charge-transfer or linker-to-metal charge-transfer (LMCT) mechanisms.[61] For example, amination is an important method and the materials constructed by the aminated linkers can absorb the light with wider wavelength range.[62,63] A ligand-to-ligand charge transfer (LLCT) process has also been proposed. Photoinduced electrons can transfer from one site of linkers to the nonadjacent site through the metal center.[64]
Most MOF materials offer large surface area (more than $1000\ \mathrm{m}^{2}\ \mathrm{g}^{-1})$ and this property endows MOF with superior adsorption capacity which is propitious to $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ reduction. Substrates can spread into the interior of materials and contact with catalytic active sites sufficiently through these pores and then reactions can take place favorably.[65] The size of pores inside can be regulated and this transformation is beneficial to the selectivity of reactants and products depended on the dimension and shape.[35] The tunable porosity also facilitates the functionalization of the materials that the guest molecules possessing catalytic ability can be immobilized into the inner cages of MOFs with less difficulty.
To fabricate the materials which are equipped with the structure mentioned above, numerous synthetic approaches have been studied including hydrothermal or solvothermal synthesis,[97,98] microwave-assisted synthesis,[99–101] sonochemical synthesis,[101–103] electrochemical synthesis,[104–106] and mechanochemical synthesis.[107,108] Among them, hydrothermal or solvothermal synthesis is the most common route. HKUST-1 and MOF-5 are two fully characterized MOFs, the energy of microwave can be utilized to synthesize materials.[109] Rapid heating and high energy utilization efficiency also make this route superior.[110] Sonochemical synthesis is an untraditional method that provides energy by ultrasonic waves based on the acoustic cavitation.[110,111] It is also notable that sono­ chemical synthesis can transfer the pristine morphology of MOFs.[103]
In 2012, Gordon et  al.[101] employed different methods to synthesize MIL-53(Fe) and compare the samples derived from the distinct circumstances by SEM images (Figure 1). It is revealed that the samples made from solvothermal method are much larger than those under microwave and ultrasound conditions. Moreover, Sabouni’s group has successfully fabricated the CPM-5 (a highly porous indium based MOF) by means of the microwave-assisted synthesis in $10\ \mathrm{min}$ . The surface area of these samples is about $2187~\mathrm{m}^{2}~\mathrm{g}^{-1}$ , which is much higher than that originated from conventional electric heating which requires several days.[112]
## 3. Mechanisms of $\mathsf{c o}_{2}$ Reduction
The reduction of abundant and inexpensive $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ into valuable products requires to overcome the barriers of thermodynamics and kinetics. Among these, $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ reduction on the basis of heterogeneous catalysts has been studied extensively and is known as a promising approach to reduce the global $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ emissions. With the heterogeneous catalysts, $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ reduction can be mainly driven by photocatalytic and electrocatalytic reactions. These two methods can be performed at mild conditions (i.e., low temperature or pressure). For this regard, studies on thermodynamics and kinetics of $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ reduction were reported mostly on the basis of photocatalysis and electrocatalysis, including a review paper on this topic by Schneider and Fujita.[113]
In terms of kinetics, the adsorption energies of reaction intermediates seem to follow linear scaling relationships. In order to improve reaction rates of MOFs on $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ reduction, breaking such linear scaling relations is required, which can be achieved by: (1) reducing the coordination numbers to generate open metal site, (2) introducing oxophilic sites, and (3) coordinating with active ligands. On the basis of $E^{\theta}$ , the reductions of $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ toward hydrocarbon products should be thermodynamically more favorable than CO, HCHO, HCOOH, and $\mathrm{H}_{2}$ production.[113] Multielectron and multiproton reduction of $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ in aqueous solutions (Figure 2 and Table 2) are affected by a lower thermodynamic barrier. $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ reduction such as the catalytic hydrogenation reaction is considered as a potential path forward for the sustainable production of carbon monoxide, methane, methanol, formate, etc.[114] Obviously, it requires to break the chemical bonds of $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ molecule first, which is a multielectron pathway. However, the activation of $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ is often challenging, owing to the high thermodynamic stability of this molecule.[115] Therefore, the activation of robust $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ molecule is kinetically a rate-determining step (RDS) in catalytic $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ reductions; for example, RDS in CO2 methanation.[116,117]
The formal redox potentials $(E^{\theta})$ for various $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ reduction reactions can be obtained from thermodynamic data and are provided in Table 2 for reference.[118,119] The reduction potential of the hydrogen evolution reaction is included because this reaction invariably competes with the electroreduction of $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ .
$\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ is a kind of stable compound due to its $\scriptstyle{\mathrm{C=O}}$ bond energy $(750\mathrm{~kJ\mol^{-1}})$ much higher than $\mathsf{C}^{\mathrm{-}\mathrm{{C}}}$ $(336\mathrm{~kJ~mol^{-1}})$ , $-0(327\mathrm{kJ}\mathrm{mol}^{-1})$ , and $\mathrm{C-H}~(411~\mathrm{kJ~mol^{-1}})$ .[120] The first step of $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ reduction is to break $\scriptstyle{\mathrm{C=O}}$ and it is an endothermal reaction, so much energy should be adsorbed and utilized rationally during the illumination. When the light introduces, electrons are generated from the valence band (VB) of material and then transfer to the lowest unoccupied molecular orbital (LUMO) of $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ to form $\mathrm{CO}_{2}\mathrm{\mathrm{^{-}}}$ . The introduced unpaired electron interacts repulsively with the electron pairs on the oxygen atoms and this lead to the bend of $\scriptstyle{\mathrm{C=O}}$ and decrease its stability. The catalytic $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ reduction consists a variety of catalytic processes including steam reforming and hydrogenation reactions. Case by case, each reaction has their own distinct thermodynamics and kinetics. For example, lower temperature or higher reaction pressure could favor the synthesis of methanol in $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ hydrogenation reaction.[121,122] Afterward, extra electrons and protons are involved to obtain various products. However, the redox potentials of each product are different such as $\scriptstyle{\mathrm{HCOOH}}/{\mathrm{CO}_{2}}$ $(-0.166~\mathrm{V},$ vs NHE), $\mathrm{CH_{4}/C O_{2}}$ $(-0.24~\mathrm{V},$ vs NHE), $\mathrm{CH}_{3}\mathrm{OH}/\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ $(-0.38\mathrm{V},$ vs NHE), and $\mathrm{CO}/\mathrm{CO}_{2}(-0.48\mathrm{V},$ vs NHE). Therefore, the VB and conduction band (CB) position of photocatalysts do not only determine if the conversion can take place, but also explicit the kind of products to a certain extent. Moreover, the transmitting efficiency of electrons between the material and $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ also influence the generated fuels because different products need different number of electrons. For example, the formation of CO needs two electrons, while $\mathrm{CH}_{4}$ needs eight.
## 3.1. $\mathsf{c o}_{2}$ Methanation Reaction
The catalytic hydrogenation of $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ to methane $\mathrm{(CH_{4})}$ , also called the Sabatier reaction, is an important catalytic process. Research into the reaction of $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ methanation is attracting a lot of interest, partly because $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ is one of the most significant greenhouse gases.[123] Another important reason is the utility of $\mathrm{CH}_{4}$ as an simple hydrocarbon, where the existing infrastructure for natural gas storage, distribution, and consumption can be utilized.[124] In the ammonia synthesis industry, $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ methanation can be used for the removal of trace $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ (or CO) to prevent catalyst poisoning.[125] Catalytic of $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ to methane is thermodynamically favorable $(\Delta G_{298\mathrm{~K~}}=-130.8\mathrm{~kJ~}\mathrm{~mol}^{-1})$ ; however, the reduction of the fully oxidized carbon to methane is an 8-electron process with significant kinetic limitations, which thus requires a catalyst to achieve acceptable rates and selectivities.[121] The most widely studied MOF-based heterogeneous catalysts for $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ methanation are Ni, Ru, and Rh metal particles supported on ZIF-67, MOF-5.[126–128]
