## 3.3. Formation of CO by $\mathsf{c o}_{2}$ Reduction
With respect to $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ reduction to fuel precursors such as CO, multielectron steps are generally more favorable than single electron reductions, as thermodynamically more stable molecules are produced. The electrochemical reduction of $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ to CO is a simple two-electron transfer process. The electron reduction of $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ to CO occurs at
$$
\boldsymbol{E}^{\theta}=-0.53\mathrm{V}
$$
A key challenge for the electrochemical CO production from $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ is the competing hydrogen evolution reaction (HER) at reduction electrode potentials, which lowers the Faradaic efficiency for CO generation. Strong adsorption and subsequent activation of $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ on the electrocatalytic sites can favor $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ reduction over the HER. There are some MOF-based heterogeneous catalysts for $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ reduction to CO extensively studied, such as $\mathrm{g}{\cdot}\mathrm{C}_{3}\mathrm{N}_{4}$ incorporated Co-ZIF-9,[68] UiO-66- $\mathrm{.C_{3}N_{4}}$ nanosheet,[89] and Ag- $\cdot{\mathrm{Re}}^{\mathrm{I}}(\mathrm{CO})_{3}$ (dcbpy),[95] etc.
## 3.4. Formate and Formic Acid Formation by $\mathsf{c o}_{2}$ Reduction
$\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ reduciton for HCOOH formation provide by a high applied potential of below $-1.5{\mathrm{~V~}}$ versus standard hydrogen electrode (SHE), which is generally needed to obtain sufficient current for the process. In addition, the presence of $\mathrm{HCO}_{3}^{-}$ ions was found to enhance $\scriptstyle{\mathrm{HCOO^{-}}}$ production. Previous study showed that the presence of a base in the reaction system supports the hydrogenation of $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ to formate.[139] Copper rubeanate MOF shows effective converstion of $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ into HCOOH due to its characteristics of electronic conductivity, nanopores, and dispersed reaction sites.[140] $\mathrm{NH}_{2}$ -MIL-125(Ti) is the first Ti-containing MOF for the photocatalytic reduction of $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ to $\mathrm{HCOO^{-}},^{[67]}$ the mechanism is similar to the generation of electrons and holes over photoexcited metal oxide semiconductors.
## 4. MOFs-Based Material for $\mathsf{c o}_{2}$ Reduction
MOFs typically contain pores of size suitable for capturing small molecules, such as carbon dioxide and water, in the cavities. Incorporating catalytic $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ reduction sites into these materials creates a favorable structure in which $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ can effectively adsorb, diffuse, and react within the porous framework. MOF linkers provide an auxiliary method to install open metal sites and molecular catalysts.
## 4.1. Photocatalytic Reduction of $\mathsf{c o}_{2}$
As we all know, the Sun is one of the most important clean energy sources and solar energy is characterized by its inexhaustibility.[141] It is fascinating to convert solar energy into chemical energy directly through $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ reduction using the relatively simple devices. The key issue of these devices is selecting suitable catalysts which can achieve the production of energy substances with high efficiency, specific selectivity, and stability.[142] So far, many semiconductors have been researched on their ability of photocatalytic $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ reduction such as $\mathrm{TiO}_{2}$ ,[143] CdS,[144] $z_{\mathrm{nO}}$ ,[145] $Z\mathrm{n}S$ ,[146] $\mathrm{BiVO_{4}},[147]$ and $\mathrm{g}{\cdot}\mathrm{C}_{3}\mathrm{N}_{4}$ .[148] However, their photocatalytic performance is far from satisfaction for practical application because of the fast combination of photoinduced carrier, the poor $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ absorption and the narrow wavelength range of absorbable light which are essential problems to improve the catalytic capability.
At the very beginning, MOFs are recognized as a kind of porous materials whose adsorption performance is much better than other known materials. Dyes,[149] drugs,[150,151] and some micromolecules[152,153] including carbon dioxide have been chosen to test their sorption capacity. These earlier researches have certified that MOFs are excellent adsorbents for $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ . Besides, it is convenient to tune the composition of MOFs by design the ligands or a postsynthetic exchange method to utilize the visible light more effectively.[154] Last but not least, there are some studies indicated that the metal clusters doping to MOFs can be considered as the semiconductor quantum dots which contribute to photocatalytic activity.[155] $\bar{\mathrm{Fe}}^{3+}$ ,[58] $\mathrm{Zr^{4+},\vec{\imath}^{77}}]$ and $\mathrm{Ti}^{4+[62]}$ which are the metal centers in the corresponding MOFs have been proved by electron spin resonance (ESR) spectroscopy studies to be the catalytically active sites where $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ molecules can acquire the photogenerated electrons. Although the MOFs have lots of advantages as photocatalyst to reduce $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ , their catalytic efficiency is not high enough to meet industrial demand and their manufacture cost is really expensive. Many studies have been taken to expand the wavelength of absorbing light and lower the combination of the photoinduced electrons and holes to promote their performance.Choose a appropriate anchor group for the photosensitization scheme is still not clear. Among the anchor groups, the most extensively used ones are ${\mathrm{-PO}}_{3}{\mathrm{H}}_{2}$ and COOH. As compared to the $-\mathrm{COOH}$ group, ${\mathrm{-PO}}_{3}\mathrm{H}_{2}$ has been demonstrated as more stable under acidic and aqueous conditions compared with the $-\mathrm{COOH}$ group. An ideal photosensitizer should absorb the light covering the entire visible region and extending to the near-IR region
## 4.1.1. MOFs-Based Materials for Metal Heterogeneous Catalysts
It is a selectable method for inhibiting the recombination of electron–hole carriers via integrating MOFs with other kind of materials when their energy band matches well with each other. Noble metals and some semiconductors are good candidates to incorporate in MOFs.
When MOFs are doped by the noble metal, the Schottky barrier will form at the junction of these two materials which is benefit to separate the electron–hole pairs. In 2014, Sun and co-workers[69] have demonstrated the different effects of Pt and Au doped ${\mathrm{NH}}_{2^{-}}{\mathrm{MIL}}{\cdot}125(\mathrm{Ti})$ on photocatalytic reaction. Two doped MOFs have been successfully prepared by the same progress called solution infiltration testified by the result of powder X-ray diffraction (XRD) and X-ray photoelectron spectroscopy (XPS), $\mathrm{Pt}/\mathrm{NH}_{2}$ -MIL-125(Ti) shows an enhanced photoreduction behavior while Au decreases the formation of formic acid. Actually, the noble metal as one of the best electron traps are in favor of hydrogen evolution, whereas this property lowers the chance of the emergence of the active sites for $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ reduction caused by the electrons transferring from the excited organic ligands to the Ti–O clusters. To elucidate the photocatalytic performance of $\mathrm{Pt}/\mathrm{NH}_{2}$ -MIL-125(Ti), ESR, and density functional theory (DFT) calculation have been applied. As shown in Figure 3a,b, when the hydrogen of the product in this photocatalytic reaction was flowed into the irradiated materials, the $\mathrm{Ti}^{3+}$ signal at $\mathrm{\bf{g}}$ value of 1.988 appeared in the spectrum of $\mathrm{Pt}/\mathrm{NH}_{2}\mathrm{-MIL}{-}125(\mathrm{Ti})$ and no signal was emerged over $\mathrm{Au}/\mathrm{NH}_{2}$ -MIL-125(Ti). Since ${\mathrm{Ti}}^{3+}$ is contributed to formate generation, this phenomenon inspired us why $\mathrm{Pt/NH}_{2}\mathrm{\cdotMIL}{\cdot}125\mathrm{(Ti)}$ has higher photocatalytic activity for $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ reduction. Not only that, the appearance of ${\mathrm{Ti}}^{3+}$ has been explained by the result of DFT calculation. In the premise that each constitutional unit contains only one noble metal atom, it is most steady when the Pt atom coordinate with one $\mathrm{~N~}$ atom and one O atom, while the Au atom occupies the bridging site of two N atoms.[69] The neighboring O atom can be hydrogenated by the $\mathrm{H}_{2}$ molecules dissociated on Pt atom and then the TiO bond will be weakened which improve the possibility of the ${\mathrm{Ti}}^{3+}$ formation. The calculation of the energy barriers of hydrogen diffusion (0.53 vs 0.94 eV) further confirms the result.
Limited by the scarcity, noble metal is not the ideal catalyst for the application in the large scale, so develop a system without noble metal is attractive. Graphitic carbon nitride $(\mathrm{g}{\cdot}\mathrm{C}_{3}\mathrm{N}_{4})$
as a kind of visible-light responding material has got more attention and has been explored for water splitting and $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ reduction. Wang et  al.[68] associated mesoporous $\mathrm{g-C}_{3}\mathrm{N}_{4}$ with Co-ZIF-9 to assemble a system containing bipyridine and triethanolamine (TEOA) which are the electron mediator and electron donor, respectively. This system produces $20.8~\upmu\mathrm{mol}$ CO and $3.3\mathrm{\textmumol\textmum}$ within two hours and the optimized quantum yield is $0.9\%$ . CO and $\mathrm{H}_{2}$ cannot be detected in the absence of $\mathrm{g-C}_{3}\mathrm{N}_{4}$ or Co-ZIF-9 proved that there is the synergistic effect between these two materials. Therefore, it is attractive to obtain the composite of $\mathrm{g-C_{3}N_{4}}$ and MOFs. In 2015, Shi et  al.[89] reported that UiO-66/carbon nitride nanosheet (CNNS) and UiO-66/bulk carbon nitride (bulk CN) has been successfully prepared. As shown in Figure 4a, the ultrasonic assisted method was employed to exfoliate the bulk CN in water, the suspension was centrifuged to achieve CNNS and then CNNS assembled with UiO-66. The morphology of prepared sample is revealed by the transmission electron microscopy (TEM) and high-resolution TEM (HRTEM) results (Figure 4b). The author believes CNNS will suppress the fast recombination of electron–hole pairs which is the feature of the bulk CN. The result of photoluminescence (PL) spectra (Figure 4c,d) supports the assumption. The peak intensity of UiO-66/CNNS is lower than that of UiO-66/bulk CN which indicated that CNNS is more favorable to separate the photoexcited electron–hole pairs which was also certified by the calculation of the average lifetime, 690.4 ns for UiO-66/bulk CN and 846.3 ns for the UiO-66/CNNS. In addition, the valence-band X-ray photoelectron spectro­ scopy (VB-XPS) manifest that the liquid exfoliation makes the band gap of CNNS enlarge. Its VB maximum is $1.68\ \mathrm{eV}$ and CB edge locates at $-1.39\mathrm{~V~}$ (vs NHE) matched well with the highest occupied molecular orbital and LUMO of UiO-66 which enhances the photocatalytic ability (Figure 4e). It can be conferred from the band gap enlargement that the photogenerated electrons are much easier to transfer from CNNS to UiO-66. Also, the soft nature of CNNS make the contact between them and MOFs more intimate which shorten the electron-transmit distance. In brief, it is an intelligent strategy to enable the connection of two components more osculating. Ultimately, a plausible mechanism (Figure 4f) has been proposed to explain the $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ photo­reduction over the UiO-66/ CNNS. When the material is irradiated, electrons generate in the VB of CNNS and transfer to UiO-66 to reduce $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ . Meanwhile, TEOA as the electron donor consumes the photoinduced holes to inhibit the recombination.
Except the CN, various semiconductors have been chosen to construct the hybrid structure with MOFs. $\mathrm{TiO}_{2}$ is most popular among them. $\mathrm{CPO}{\cdot}27{\cdot}\mathrm{Mg}/\mathrm{TiO}_{2}$ has been prepared by Wang et al.[156] to investigate its photoredox capability. The as-prepared product has been characterized by XRD, SEM, TEM, and TG to certificate the successful acquirement. It has been revealed in the UV/vis DRS spectra (Figure 5a) that $\mathrm{TiO}_{2}$ is intimately contact with CPO-27-Mg. There are three main absorptions ranging from 200 to $600\mathrm{nm}$ in which the first and second peaks are attributed to the property of pure $\mathrm{TiO}_{2}$ and CPO-27-Mg. In addition, the third shoulder peak which cannot be observed in the UV/vis DRS spectra of the raw materials is the most important because it should be ascribed to the coordination between the O atom in the linker and Ti atom in the $\mathrm{TiO}_{2}$ which make the electron transport more efficient. CPO-27-Mg has been selected due to its high $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ uptake and the alkaline metal active sites $(\mathrm{Mg}^{2+})$ that are in large proportion. To demonstrate the effect of $\mathrm{Mg}^{2+}$ , the reference experiments have been conducted. On the one hand, the consolidation of MOFs into $\mathrm{TiO}_{2}$ reduces the production of hydrogen (Figure 5b); on the other hand, the $\mathrm{ZIF}{\cdot}8/\mathrm{TiO}_{2}$ whose ability of $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ absorption is similar has been picked. It founded that $40.9~\upmu\mathrm{mol}$ $\mathbf{g}^{-1}$ CO and $23.5\ \upmu\mathrm{mol}\ \mathrm{g}^{-1}\ \mathrm{CH}_{4}$ were produced over $\mathrm{CPO}{\cdot}27{\cdot}\mathrm{Mg}/\mathrm{TiO}_{2}$ while the value over $\mathrm{ZIF}{\cdot}8/\mathrm{TiO}_{2}$ is $29.8\ \upmu\mathrm{mol}\ \mathrm{g}^{-1}\ \mathrm{CO}$ and $14.7\upmu\mathrm{mol}\ \mathrm{g}^{-1}\mathrm{CH}_{4}$ . It can be concluded that the open alkaline metal sites are able to stimulate the $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ photoreduction and inhibit the competitive reaction (hydrogen formation) due to lowering the concentration of $\mathrm{H^{+}}$ .
Recently, one complex semiconductor, $\mathrm{Cd}_{x}\mathrm{Zn}_{1-x}\mathrm{S}$ , has been examined to grasp its effect in the mixed system.[79] As a kind of solid solution, the band gap of $\mathrm{Cd}_{x}\mathrm{Zn}_{1-x}\mathrm{S}$ could be regulated more convenient than CdS and $Z\mathrm{ns}$ . Among this category, $\mathrm{Cd}_{0.2}\mathrm{Zn}_{0.8}\mathrm{S}$ exhibit the best photocatalytic activity, so a series of $\mathrm{Cd}_{0.2}\mathrm{Zn}_{0.8}\mathrm{S@UiO-66-NH}_{2}$ composites has been fabricated by altering the content of $\mathrm{UiO}{-}66{\cdot}\mathrm{NH}_{2}$ . The most crucial work in this paper is to identify the band gap of the pristine materials through the following equation
$$
\alpha h\nu=A\big(h\nu–E_{\mathrm{g}}\big)^{n/2}
$$
$\alpha,\ h,\ \nu,\ A_{\mathrm{{z}}}$ and $E_{\mathrm{g}}$ in the formula represent the absorption coefficient, Planck constant, the light frequency, a constant, and the band gap. The value of $n$ is determined if the optical transition of the semiconductor is direct.
Besides, the band position of $\mathrm{Cd}_{0.2}\mathrm{Zn}_{0.8}\mathrm{S}$ and UiO- $66\mathrm{-NH}_{2}$ can be achieved by the formula given below
$$
\begin{array}{r}{E_{\mathrm{VB}}=X-E^{\mathrm{e}}+0.5E_{\mathrm{g}}}\end{array}
$$
$$
E_{\mathrm{CB}}=E_{\mathrm{VB}}-E_{\mathrm{g}}
$$
where $\scriptstyle{E_{\mathrm{VB}}}$ and $E_{\mathrm{CB}}$ are valence band and conduction band edge potential, $E^{\mathrm{e}}$ represents the value of free electron’s energy on the hydrogen scale, $E_{\mathrm{g}}$ is the band gap energy, and $X$ is equal to the electronegativity of the semiconductor. The final estimated data is shown in Table 3.
As shown in Figure 6a, the electrons generated on the HOMO of $\mathrm{UiO}{-}66{\cdot}\mathrm{NH}_{2}$ can transmit to the conduction band of $\mathrm{Cd}_{0.2}\mathrm{Zn}_{0.8}\mathrm{S}$ which is contributed to suppress the recombination of light-induced electron–hole pairs. The results of PL spectroscopy (Figure 6b) and the transient photo­current response (Figure 6c) further verify this viewpoint. The as-fabricated $\mathrm{Cd}_{0.2}\mathrm{Zn}_{0.8}\mathrm{S@UiO{-}66{-}N H}_{2}$ samples with $20\mathrm{\wt\%}$ $\mathrm{UiO}{-}66{\cdot}\mathrm{NH}_{2}$ $(\mathsf{C}\mathsf{Z}\mathsf{S}\ @\mathsf{U}\mathsf{N}20)$ possess the lowest peak intensity and highest photocurrent density, indicating the efficient photoinduced charge separation within the composite material.
In order to reduce the reaction temperature for $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ reduction, Zeng et  al. inspired by the light-to-heat conversion for nanostructures, and encapsulated Pt nanocubes and Au nano­ cages into a zeolitic imidazolate framework (ZIF-8), Au&Pt $@$ ZIF.[157] The heat generated by the relaxation of excited plasmonic NPs could significantly contribute to the enhancement of $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ hydrogenation (Figure $\mathsf{7a-c})$ .
Au&Pt@ZIF achieved the highest TOF number of $1522~\mathrm{h}^{-1}$ under light irradiation, 13 times as high as that in the dark, while the TOF numbers of other catalysts were ${\approx}100\ \mathrm{h}^{-1}$ with/ without light irradiation (Figure 7c). They also proposed that if the surface of catalysts is locally heated, the reaction temperature for $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ hydrogenation will be efficiently reduced.
High catalytic activity was resulted by the heat generated from the photothermal effect of Au nanocages, which was mainly insulated in the ZIF-8 to improve the catalytic activity of $\mathrm{Pt}$ nanocubes. It provides severe study for the metal nanocrystals/MOF hybrid structures for a vatiety of catalytic reactions.[157]
Besides the integration of photothemal effect and heat insulation by prepaing metal $\textcircled{a}Z\mathrm{IF}$ , the latest research by Zhang and co-workers demonstrated that the catalytic active metal center can cooperate with neighboring hydroxide ligands to boost the photocatalytic $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ reduction.[158] A series of isostructural/ isoreticular honeycomb-like MOFs, such as $[\mathsf{C o}_{2}(\mathsf{\upmu}\mathrm{-}\mathrm{Cl})_{2}(\mathsf{b b t a})]$ (MAF-X27-Cl, $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{bbta}~=~1\mathrm{H}$ , 5H-benzo-(1,2-d:4,5-d′) bistriazole), $\left[\mathrm{Co}_{2}(\upmu\mathrm{-}\mathrm{OH})_{2}(\mathrm{bbta})\right]$ (MAF-X27-OH), and $\big[\mathrm{Co}_{2}(\mathrm{dobdc})\big]$ $(\mathrm{MOF{-}74{-}C o,H_{4}d o b d c{=}2,5}.$ dihydroxyl–1,4-benzenedicarboxylic acid) with different donor atoms were synthesized. CO and $\mathrm{H}_{2}$ were detected as the main product and byproduct (Figure 8a–f) for these MOFs. They illustrated that the $\upmu{\mathrm{-}}\mathrm{OH}^{-}$ ligands can cooperate with catalytic active metal centers in MOFs, serving as hydrogen-bonding donors and local proton sources to improve the performance of photocatalytic $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ reduction. MAF-X27l-OH shows much higher catalytic performances than for all reported heterogeneous catalysts working at similar reaction conditions,[158] in both pure $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ (CO selectivities of $98.2\%$ and TOFs of $0.059~\mathrm{s^{-1}}$ ) and 0.1 atm $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ (CO selectivities of $95.1\%$ and TOFs of $0.021\ \mathrm{s}^{-1}$ ). Periodic DFT was used to understand the roles of the $\scriptstyle{\upmu\mathrm{-OH}}$ ligands. In the optimized structures, the $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ binding strength of MAF-X27-OH is stronger than that for MAF-X27-Cl. Furthermore, the $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ molecule formed strong H-bonds with the two $\upmu\mathrm{-}\mathrm{OH}^{-}$ ligands neighboring the metal center. However, the $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}$ binding of MAF-X27-OH is significantly weaker compared with its $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ binding. Strong $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ binding affinity can stabilize the initial ${\mathrm{Co}}{\mathrm{-CO}}_{2}$ adduct to promote the $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ reduction, weak $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}$ binding affinity can reduce the undesired metal ion– $\mathrm{\cdotH}_{2}\mathrm{O}$ adduct which lead to the $\mathrm{H}_{2}$ byproduct. Meanwhile, the $\upmu\mathrm{-}\mathrm{OH}^{-}$ ligands can donate a proton to participate the photocatalytic reaction. New MOF with high catalytic performances could be prepared under this strategy and open up new possibilities for $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ reduction materials.
On the basis of the above examples, it is the most important to enlighten the energy band relationship of materials in hybrid systems. The suitable staggered relationship is the main reason that the photoinduced charges can transmit between MOFs and semiconductors. The intimate contact also plays the role that cannot be ignored. It contributes to accelerating the transport of electrons/holes and suppressing the recombination.
## 4.1.2. Morphology Design of MOFs
Although the incorporation of the suitable materials can elevate the photocatalytic activity of MOFs, it still cannot satisfy the requirement. A great deal of action has been taken to ameliorate the hybrid system involving the morphology design. The composition of metal centers, organic ligand, and the morphology of MOFs all play an important role in the catalytic efficiency. The morphology of materials can tune their response in the visible-light region, take full advantage of the incident light, make the components contact more intimately and scale down the dimension of photocatalysts which is beneficial to suppress the recombination of electron–hole pairs. A hierarchical morphology MOF catalyst can show improved performance due to the efficient transfer of photogenerated electrons to the reaction sites. The increase of the contact area between materials and $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ was also observed.
Liu et  al.[78] synthesized the grain-like $\mathrm{Zn}_{2}\mathrm{GeO}_{4}/\mathrm{ZIF}{-}8$ nanorods by two steps to photoreduct $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ to methanol (Figure 9). As shown in Figure 9a, the incorporation of ZIF-8 into $Z\mathrm{n}_{2}\mathrm{GeO}_{4}$ is capable to promote the gas uptake of the compounds. Another experiment over the $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ aqueous solution exhibits the adsorptive behavior of the samples. $\boldsymbol{0.5\mathrm{~g~}}$ ZIF-8, $\mathrm{Zn}_{2}\mathrm{GeO}_{4}/\mathrm{ZIF}{-}8$ or $\mathrm{Zn}_{2}\mathrm{GeO}_{4}$ was putted into $50~\mathrm{mL}$ of the $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ aqueous solution and stirred for $1.5\mathrm{{h}}$ and then the concentration of $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ was detected to be 20.2, 60.3, and $123.4~\mathrm{mg~L^{-1}}$ , respectively, in accordance with the result of gas sorption. In addition, the images of SEM and TEM (Figure 9b,c) have uncovered the morphology that ZIF-8 nanoparticles are discrete along the $\mathrm{Zn}_{2}\mathrm{GeO}_{4}$ nanorods and the diameter of ZIF-8 is about $10\ \mathrm{nm}$ much smaller than the $\mathrm{TiO}_{2}$ in $_\mathrm{CPO-27-Mg/TiO_{2}}$ (300 to $500\ \mathrm{nm}$ ) mentioned above. It is meaningful because the shrink of magnitude improve the distribution of the photogenerated charge. The preparation process of $\mathrm{Zn}_{2}\mathrm{GeO}_{4}/\mathrm{ZIF}{-}8$ nanorods is illustrated in Figure 9d.
The hybrid of MOFs and $\mathrm{TiO}_{2}$ can also be equipped with regular morphology. The SEM and the TEM images (Figure 10a,b) suggests that $\mathrm{HKUST}{\cdot}1@\mathrm{TiO}_{2}$ core–shell structures have been attained and the experiment with two steps has been developed to synthesize this kind of material.[86] First, HKUST-1 crystals were prepared with $\mathrm{Cu}_{2}\mathrm{O}$ precursors and the assistance of polyvinylpyrrolidone (PVP). Next, a hydrolysis method was employed to decorate $\mathrm{TiO}_{2}$ on the surface of HKUST-1. The control experiment without PVP reveals that it is necessary for the conformation of the core–shell structure to add PVP, but the reason was not mention in the paper. The most surprising part is that the $\mathrm{TiO}_{2}$ shell does not have a strong impact on the gas absorption ability of MOFs proved by the gas-sorption measurements (Figure 10c,d). The total surface area of bare HKUST-1 is $1183~\mathrm{~m}^{2}~\mathrm{g}^{-1}$ compared to the $756~\mathrm{~m}^{2}~\mathrm{g}^{-1}$ of the same weight composite. Given the $\mathrm{TiO}_{2}$ accounting for one third of the total weight, the decrease is acceptable. Apart from the absorption ability, the capability of electron transport has been testified by ultrafast transient absorption (TA) spectroscopy. A typical recovery time of pure $\mathrm{TiO}_{2}$ is about 20 ps and no TA signal can be collected over HKUST-1, while the compound possess the 500 ps of the recovery time indicating the photoinduced excitons in this system have longer life time. At last, it should be emphasized that the photocatalyst, $\mathrm{TiO}_{2}$ , can be exposed completely in this system and it is easier to utilize the light adequately and motivate more excitons due to the low efficiency of MOFs in photogenerating electrons and holes.
Except the hybrid materials, the pure MOFs also can present a hierarchical nanostructure. The flower-like bifunctional
Ru-MOF, $(\mathrm{Cd}_{2}[\mathrm{Ru(dcbpy)}_{3}]\cdot12\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O})\mathrm{n}$ $(\mathrm{dcbpy}=2,2^{\prime}$ -bipyridine$^{4,4^{\prime}}$ -dicarboxylate), has been successfully fabricated in 2015, but there is no analogous report in recent two years.[71] Microscale nanoflower architecture (Figure 11a,b) can be acquired by an uncomplicated method which is to adjust the concentration of reactants. The microcrystals and bulk crystals of Ru-MOF have also been synthesized to compare with the nanoflowers about the photocatalytic capability. It can be seen form Figure 11c that the Ru-MOF nanoflowers are more active than those of the microcrystal and bulk crystal. The outstanding performance of ${\mathrm{Ru-MOF}}$ nanoflowers on photoreduction may originate from the following three sections. First, the structure of nanoflower can extend the visible-light absorption and the absorption fringe is about $750~\mathrm{nm}$ compared to $650~\mathrm{nm}$ over the metalloligand $[\mathrm{Ru(H_{2}d c b p y)_{3}}]\cdot\mathrm{Cl_{2}}$ (Figure 11d). Second, the luminescence decay curve (Figure 11e,f) reveals that the exciton lifetime of the Ru-MOF is $5.49~\upmu\mathrm{s}$ that is much longer than the metalloligand $(\approx483$ ns). Finally, the BET surface area, as an important parameter, has been obtained and the nanoflowers equip larger area $(8.08\ \mathrm{m}^{2}\ \mathrm{g}^{-1})$ than the microcrystals $(1.33~\mathrm{m}^{2}~\mathrm{g}^{-1})$ . A tentative mechanism is manifested in Figure 11g. Upon the light irradiating, $\mathtt{R u}$ ions generate charges, holes are vanished by TEOA and electrons transfer to the pyridine rings of $\mathrm{dcbpy^{2-}}$ ligands. The absorbed $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ receive two electrons from the neighboring pyridine rings to form formate.
For MOFs, the adjustment of the microcosmic crystal type may cause the change of macroscopic morphology. Three copper(II) imidazolate frameworks with different crystalline phase have been produced in the presence of the same precursors.[159] The sample with pink (P), blue (J), and green color (G) was attained by adding NaOH, ${\mathrm{NaHCO}}_{3}$ , and $\mathrm{NH_{4}O H}$ , respectively. The XRD patterns of as-prepared sample is depicted in Figure 12a and it is easy to conclude that G owns orthorhombic architecture and J shows monoclinic phase, while no structural model can be found to assort with P. However, the comparison of SEM and TEM images of different samples has not been carried on in this paper. As presented in Figure 12b, the photocatalytic performance of G is superior to J and P. To clarify the reason, UV–vis absorption spectra (Figure 12c) has been used to probe the optical quality of these MOFs. Among them, G displays the strongest visible-light absorption followed by J and $\mathrm{\DeltaP}$ in consistent with the color of themselves and the narrowest band gap $(2.49\ \mathrm{eV})$ calculated by the formula above. The result of nitrogen adsorption studies is also positive that the BET surface areas of P, G, and J are 11.92, 3.99, and $0.68~\mathrm{m}^{2}~\mathrm{g}^{-1}$ suggesting the $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ adsorption of G would not be too bad.
## 4.1.3. Changes in Interior Chemical Constitution
Obviously, it is dissatisfied to modify MOFs only in the external aspect to promote their photocatalytic property. Many efforts have been focused on the interior chemical constitution including two main measures which are doping the metal active sites and regulating the ligands.
First of all, the metal doping will increase the number of active sites, improve the ability on trapping photogenerated charges and make the light response of system much better. Also, this strategy can strengthen the absorption of $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ . Postsynthetic modifications (PSMs) is one of the most common method to endow MOFs with more well defined catalytic sites and its reaction conditions are mild enough to preserve the frameworks.
As illuminated by the effect of $\mathrm{TiO}_{2}$ and $\mathrm{Ti}^{3+}$ active sites in Ti-MOFs, this metal has been elected to modify MOFs. $\mathrm{NH}_{2}$ - ${\mathrm{UiO}}{\cdot}66({\mathrm{Zr}})$ has been chosen to fabricate this kind of material because of its stability and recyclability.[160] As-prepared $\mathrm{NH}_{2}.$ ${\mathrm{UiO}}{\cdot}66({\mathrm{Zr}})$ was immersed in the DMF solution of $\mathrm{TiCl}_{4}(\mathrm{THF})_{2}$ via the postsynthetic exchange (PSE) method and the incubation temperature and time have been investigated. As presented in Figure 13a, the higher temperature and longer time assist to achieve the composites containing more $\mathrm{\DeltaTi}$ atom, so $\mathrm{NH}_{2}$ -
Uio- $66(\mathrm{Zr/Ti})$ -T-t is used for short where $T$ stands for the temperature and $t$ represents the time. The XRD patterns and $\mathrm{N}_{2}$ adsorption/desorption isotherms manifest that the $\mathrm{NH}_{2}$ -UiO$66(\mathrm{Zr})$ is not collapsed after the incubation. The Ti 2p region of the X-ray photoelectron spectrum (XPS) has been depicted in Figure 13b and two peaks can be observed at 458.7 and $464.5\ \mathrm{eV}$ in accordance to Ti $2\mathrm{p}_{3/2}$ and Ti $2\mathrm{p}_{1/2}$ indicating the true substitution of Ti. The stronger and wider light absorption shown in the UV–visible spectra (Figure 13c) and the higher $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ uptake $(83~\mathrm{cm}^{3}~\mathrm{g}^{-1}$ of $\mathrm{NH_{2}}\mathrm{.UiO}{\mathrm{.66(Zr/Ti)}}{\cdot}120{\cdot}16$ and $68~\mathrm{cm}^{3}~\mathrm{g}^{-1}$ of $\mathrm{NH}_{2^{-}}\mathrm{UiO}{\cdot}66(\mathrm{Zr}))$ support the phenomenon that the photoreduction performance of $\mathrm{NH_{2}{\cdot}U i O{-}}66(\mathrm{Zr/Ti}){\cdot}120{\cdot}16$ is better. In addition, the reason has been provided via the DFT calculation and in situ low temperature ESR analyses. The band structure (Figure 13d,e) of $\mathrm{NH}_{2^{-}}\mathrm{UiO}{-}66(\mathrm{Zr})$ and $\mathrm{NH}_{2}$ -UiO$66(\mathrm{Zr/Ti})$ reveals that the Ti dopant leads to the alteration of the conduction band which means the photoinduced electrons can transfer from the excited linkers to the Ti sites to form ${\mathrm{Ti}}^{3+}$ . Meanwhile, in situ low temperature ESR analyses (Figure 13f,g) illustrates that the $\mathrm{Zr^{3+}}$ signal $(\boldsymbol{\mathrm{g}}$ value at 2.002) of $\mathrm{NH}_{2}$ -UiO$66(\mathrm{Zr})$ appears after the irradiation and unchanges in the dark, but the peak intensity of $\mathrm{NH}_{2}{\cdot}\mathrm{UiO}{\cdot}66(\mathrm{Zr}/\mathrm{Ti})$ varies once the light was turned off. The intensity of $\mathrm{Ti}^{3+}$ signal $(\boldsymbol{\mathrm{g}}$ value at 1.944) decreases and that of $\mathrm{Zr^{3+}}$ increases gradually indicating that Ti as a mediator can further spread electrons to ${\mathrm{Zr^{4+}}}$ sites to prolong the lifetime of the excitons and increase the number of the active sites. In brief, electron generates by the excitation of the ligands and transfer to the metal sites. If $\mathrm{Ti^{4+}}$ receives an electron, the ${\mathrm{Ti}}^{3+}$ will appear and $\mathrm{Ti^{3+}{-}O{-}Z r^{4+}}$ bridge will form by which ${\mathrm{Ti}}^{3+}$ can donate electrons to ${\mathrm{Zr}}^{4+}$ (Figure 13h).
Co has also been investigated to modify MOFs to boost $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ reduction. A kind of prophyrin-based MOF, MOF-525 formulated as $\mathrm{Zr}_{6}\mathrm{O}_{4}(\mathrm{OH})_{4}(\mathrm{TCPP}\cdot\mathrm{H}_{2})_{3}$ $[\mathrm{TCPP}~=~4,4^{\prime},4^{\prime\prime},4^{\prime\prime\prime}$ -(porphyrin-5,10,15,20-tetrayl) tetrabenzoate], has been selected and MOF-525-Co has been synthesized by soaking in the cobalt nitrate solution (Figure 14a).[77] The Co K-edge extended X-ray absorption fine structure (EXAFS) was studied to identify if the Co atom coordinates with other atoms in the frameworks. The EXAFS Fourier transforms (Figure 14b) reveal the presence of the $\mathrm{Co-N}$ bonds and EXAFS curve fitting analysis confirm that an isolated Co atom coordinates with $3.9\mathrm{~N~}$ atoms evidencing the existence of the unsaturated active sites. For comparison, MOF-525-Zn has been obtained to extrude the effect of Co. The yield of photocatalytic product over MOF-525-Co is much higher than that of MOF-525-Zn and MOF-525. The CO evolution rate of MOF-525-Co is $200.6~{\upmu\mathrm{mol}}~\mathrm{g}^{-1}~\mathrm{h}^{-1}$ compared to $111.7~{\upmu\mathrm{mol}}~\mathrm{g}^{-1}~\mathrm{h}^{-1}$ of MOF-525-Zn and $64.02~\mathrm{\upmumol~g^{-1}~h^{-1}}$ of MOF-525, in agreement with the $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ adsorption behavior of these samples. As shown in Figure 14c, the incident-photonto-current conversion efficiency was monitored to comprehend the instantaneous electron transport. The peak of MOF-525-Co is much more intense than that of MOF-525-Zn and MOF525 that partially explains the distinguished catalytic activity of MOF-525-Co according to the calculation of energy transfer efficiency $(\Phi_{\mathrm{ET}})$ that the $\Phi_{\mathrm{ET}}$ of MOF-525-Co is bigger. Contrary to the $\mathrm{NH}_{2}{\mathrm{-UiO}}{\mathrm{-}}66(\mathrm{Zr}/\mathrm{Ti})$ talked above, there is no ESR signal of $\mathrm{Zr^{3+}}$ in MOF-525-Co, so Co cannot be a bridge to transfer electrons.
Cu doping can raise the $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ capture of the primary materials[161] and it has been report that the $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ uptake as well as the photocatalytic activity of ZIF-67 can be promoted by doping $\mathrm{{Cu}}$ ,[162] so it is reasonable to incorporate $\mathtt{C u}$ into MOFs to fabricate the functional materials. Liu et  al.[163] successfully synthesized the $\mathrm{{Cu}}$ -doping MOF $\ensuremath{(\mathrm{S}_{\mathrm{Cu}})}$ attested by the XRD patterns and SEM images (Figure 15a,b). As shown in Figure 15c, Cu atoms coordinate with four N atoms from different pyrrole rings. The MOF substrate denoted as $S_{\mathrm{{p}}}$ was attained by heating the mixture of $\mathrm{AlCl}_{3}$ · $6\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}$ and TCPP. The BET surface areas of $\ensuremath{\mathrm{s}}_{\mathrm{cu}}$ and $S_{\mathrm{p}}$ can be calculated using nitrogen adsorption–desorption isotherms to be 932 and $1187~\mathrm{cm}^{2}~\mathrm{g}^{-1}$ , while $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ adsorption over $\ensuremath{\mathrm{S}}_{\mathrm{Cu}}$ is $277.4\ \mathrm{mg}\ \mathrm{g}^{-1}$ much higher than that of $S_{\mathrm{p}}$ ( $153.1\mathrm{~mg~g^{-1}})$ . It proposes that the surface area is not parallel with the $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ capture ability. Apart from the absorption, the hysteresis loop of desorption process only emerges over $\ensuremath{\mathrm{S}}_{\mathrm{Cu}}$ indicating the chemical interaction between $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ and $\ensuremath{\mathrm{s}}_{\mathrm{cu}}$ which is also revealed in Fourier-transform infrared spectroscopy (FTIR) spectra (Figure 15d). After adding $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ , three new peaks at 1780, 1656, and $1225~\mathrm{cm}^{-1}$ appeared in the presence of $\ensuremath{\mathrm{S}}_{\mathrm{Cu}}$ and no changes happened over $S_{\mathrm{{p}}}$ . As a result, the methanol evolution rate of $\ensuremath{\mathrm{s}}_{\mathrm{cu}}$ $(262.6~\mathrm{ppm~g^{-1}~h^{-1}})$ is 7 times higher than that of $S_{\mathrm{{p}}}$ $37.5\ \mathrm{ppm\g^{-1}h^{-1}})$ .
However, it is difficult to introduce extra metal sites into some classic MOFs due to the lack of coordinative sites or the collapse of the frameworks when the instinct metal was substituted. Lee et al.[90] have solved this problem via PSE. As presented in Figure 16a, the as-prepared UiO-66 was incubated in the solution of 2,3-dihydroxyterephthalic acid (catbdc) to produce UiO66-CAT. Next, the metalation was conducted by means of the immersion into aqueous $\mathrm{K_{2}C r O_{4}}$ or $\mathrm{Ga}(\mathrm{NO}_{3})_{3}(\mathrm{H}2\mathrm{O})_{x}$ to attain UiO-66-CrCAT and UiO-66-GaCAT. The XRD patterns manifest that the crystal structure of UiO-66 retained after these disposals. UiO-66-CrCAT shows broader visible-light sorption than UiO-66-GaCAT (Figure 16b) in accordance with the deeper color of UiO-66-CrCAT. This phenomenon may result from the LMCT process between $\mathrm{Cr}^{\mathrm{III}}$ and the linkers proved by PL spectra that the peak intensity of UiO-66-CrCAT is lowest (Figure 16c). So, UiO-66-CrCAT produced $51.73\pm2.64~\upmu\mathrm{mol}$ HCOOH in 6 h more than the MOF containing Ga.
Not only the metal, the metal complex or salt can be incorporated into MOFs to gain the novel materials. It is advisable to scan the former researches to find the promising metal complex with catalytic activity. For example, $\mathrm{Mn(bpy)(CO)_{3}B r}$ (bpy $\mathbf{\Sigma}=\mathbf{\Sigma}$ $^{2,2^{\prime}}$ -bipyridine) has been investigated to reduce $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ as an electrocatalyst,[164] and then, Fei et al.[91] were inspired and tried to combine this salt with MOFs. Because of the low thermal stability, the mild PSM has been employed and the synthesized reactants of MOFs have been altered. The synthesis of UiO-67- $\mathrm{Mn(bpy)(CO)_{3}B r}$ is depicted in Figure $17\mathrm{a}$ , the solution comprising 1:1 molar ratio of $^{2,2^{\prime}}$ -bipyridine- $^{.5,5^{\prime}}$ -dicarboxylic acid $\left(\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{bpydc}\right)$ and $^{4,4^{\prime}}$ -biphenyldicarboxylic acid $(\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{bpdc})$ was involved to fabricate UiO-67-bpydc which contains coordination sites. Then the as-prepared MOF was added to the diethyl ether solution of Bromopentacarbonylmanganese(I) $[\mathrm{Mn}(\mathrm{CO})_{5}\mathrm{Br}]$ maintaining for $24\mathrm{~h~}$ and the solid was collected by centrifugation. The crystallinity of these samples was confirmed by powder XRD (Figure 17b) and the successful incorporation of $\mathrm{Mn}(\mathrm{bpy})$
make sure that the active substance will stay in MOFs until the framework disintegrates, it is a judicious choice to construct the frameworks using the metalloligands instead of common organic ligands.
The metalloligands based on Ru are preferred due to its excellent photocatalytic ability. Zhang et  al.[73] have constructed two MOFs (denoted as Ru-MOF 1 and 2) with two Rubased complexs. The models of Ru-MOF 1 and 2 are exhibited in Figure 18a,b. It can be seen that Ru-MOF 1 possesses 1D channels surrounded by enclosed wall while the interpenetrated conformation is constructed in Ru-MOF 2. The photocatalytic applications of 1 and 2 have been evaluated and the results are shown in Figure 18c. The HCOOH generating rates are similar in the beginning, but the photocatalytic activity of Ru-MOF 1 decreases dramatically with the extension of the reaction time. The crystalline states and morphologies of two samples have been monitored by XRD patterns and SEM images (Figure 18d,e). The sheet-like Ru-MOF 1 was cracked into small particles after irradiation in accordance with the XRD patterns; nevertheless, Ru-MOF maintained its morphology which contributes to the stable HCOOH formation. Although the formate produced over these materials is $\approx17\:\upmu\mathrm{mol}$ in $^{6\mathrm{{h}}}$ much more than $\mathrm{MOF}{-}253{\mathrm{-}}\mathrm{Ru}(\mathrm{CO})_{2}\mathrm{Cl}_{2}$ mentioned above, Ru-MOF 2 is more outstanding due to its stability caused by the interpenetrated structure.
$(\mathrm{CO})_{3}\mathrm{Br}$ was demonstrated by FT-IR spectra (Figure 17c) due to the appearance of new peaks (2031 and $1940\ \mathrm{cm^{-1}}$ ) caused by CO stretching vibration. It is consistent with the phenomenon that the BET area of $\mathrm{UiO-67-Mn(bpy)(CO)_{3}B r}$ shrinks because of the steric block. Turnover numbers (TONs) of formate production over $\mathrm{UiO-67-Mn(bpy)(CO)_{3}B r}$ reached $110\pm13$ in $^{18\mathrm{~h~}}$ larger than those over $\mathrm{Mn(bpy)(CO)_{3}B r}$ and $\mathrm{Mn(bpydc)(CO)_{3}B r}$ $(70\pm7.6\$ and $57\pm7.8)$ ). It is because that UiO-67 can increase $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ adsorption and inhibit the dimerization of Mn complex which will happen when these molecules catch the electron.
Ru complexes have been chosen to compose the photocatalyst with MOFs as well.[92] $\mathrm{MOF}{-}253{\mathrm{-}}\mathrm{Ru}(\mathrm{CO})_{2}\mathrm{Cl}_{2}$ has been synthesized through adding MOF-253 into the solution that contains $[\mathrm{Ru(CO)}_{3}\mathrm{Cl}_{2}]_{2}$ and refluxing. For comparison, $\mathrm{Ru}(\mathrm{bpy})_{2}\mathrm{Cl}_{2}$ has also been used to sensitize $\mathrm{MOF}{-}253{\mathrm{-}}\mathrm{Ru}(\mathrm{CO})_{2}\mathrm{Cl}_{2}$ because of its optical performance. The sensitized $\mathrm{MOF}{-}253{\mathrm{-}}\mathrm{Ru}(\mathrm{CO})_{2}\mathrm{Cl}_{2}$ shows stronger sorption of visible lights proved by UV–vis spectra and produced more HCOOH $(4.84~\upmu\mathrm{mol})$ than pure $\mathrm{MOF}{\cdot}253{\cdot}\mathrm{Ru}(\mathrm{CO})_{2}\mathrm{Cl}_{2}$ $(0.67~\mathrm{\upmumol})$ in $^{8\mathrm{~h~}}$ under the same condition. This result can be interpreted by the report in 1985 that Hawecker et  al. explicated the photoreduction activity of $[\mathrm{Ru(bpy)}_{3}]^{2+}$ .[165] Although the Ru salts can remarkably improve the yield of HCOOH, the stability is not good that $9.3\%$ of Ru leaked into the solution after $^{8\mathrm{~h~}}$ of irradiation. As a result, to
Early in 2011, six kinds of UiO-67 have been synthesized by heating the combinations of $\mathrm{ZrCl_{4}}$ , para-biphenyldicarboxylic acid (bpdc) and diverse metalloligands.[66] The crystalline of the frameworks remains owing to the approximate lengths of bpdc and metalloligands. Among them, MOF 4 which is constructed by $\mathrm{Re(CO)}_{3}(\mathrm{dcbpy)}$ Cl can photoreduce $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ to CO. The TONs of MOF 4 reached 10.9 in $20\mathrm{~h~}$ compared to 2.5 and 7.0 of $\mathrm{Re}(\mathrm{CO})_{3}(\mathrm{dcbpy})\mathrm{Cl}$ and $\mathrm{Re}(\mathrm{CO})_{3}(\mathrm{bpy})\mathrm{Cl}$ indicating that it is rational to attain the excellent photocatalysts by incorporating metal complexes into MOFs as a kind of linkers.
Ir complexes have also been investigated and been deemed to have photocatalytic potential due to the strong light absorption and the long-lived excited states.[166] Depending on this research, the Li’s group has used $\mathrm{Ir(ppy)}_{2}(\mathrm{Hdcbpy)}$ (ppy $=\ 2$ -phenylpyridine) to fabricate a coordination polymer $\mathrm{(Y[Ir(ppy)_{2}(d c b p y)]_{2}[O H]}$ , Ir-CP).[87] The crystalline structure of Ir-CP was explored by X-ray single crystal diffraction (Figure 19a) and it can be found that the neighbouring aromatic rings are about $3.5\mathring{\mathrm{A}}$ away from each other which is the feature of the $\pi{-}\pi$ stacking interaction resulting in the higher stability. As presented in Figure 19b, Ir-CP can harvest more visible light which is stem from the metal-to-ligand charge transfer and LLCT of Ir unit. A research on the luminescence decay reveals that the lifetime of the excitons in Ir-CP is $29.05~\upmu\mathrm{s}$ longer than $7.8~\upmu\mathrm{s}$ for the Ir unit. These merits lead to the improvement of photocatalytic performance (Figure 19c). Furthermore, only $3\%$ of Ir was detected in the supernatant after reaction determined by inductively coupled plasma optical emission spectrometry (ICP-OES) in agreement with the stability of this system. The result of DFT calculation is similar between Ir-CP and Ir unit suggesting that there is no energy transferring from excited Ir ligands to $\Upsilon^{3+}$ which may result from the energy level mismatch of two components. Last, a possible mechanism has been proposed shown in Figure 19d. Ir unit in MOF was excited under irradiation and then $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ received one electron from each adjacent $\mathrm{[Ir(ppy)_{2}(d c b p y)]_{2}}$ unit owning to the short distance between each pyridine ring.
Notably, the metalloligands do not only increase the number of active sites which is one of the advantages of metal doping, but also improve the stability. By means of changing the length and coordination number of metalloligands, the interior coordination environment of MOFs can be tuned and the interpenetrated structure can be formed to stabilize the frameworks and prolong the service life.
Without considering the design of metalloligands, various ligands with no metal have been fabricated to build different frameworks. These modified or completely new ligands embrace many functions such as broadening the visible-light sorption, promoting the $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ uptake, increasing the active sites and so on. Among them, amination is the simplest method. In 2012, Yanghe et  al.[62] have successfully synthesized an amine-functionalized Ti-MOF $[\mathrm{NH}_{2}\mathrm{-MIL}\cdot125(\mathrm{Ti})]$ via introducing $\mathrm{NH}_{2}$ groups into benzene-1,4-dicarboxylate. The XRD patterns (Figure 20a) certify that the incorporation of amino does not influence the structure of pristine MOF and the emerging peak at 3388 and $3512~\mathrm{cm}^{-1}$ in diffuse-reflectance infrared spectroscopy of ${\mathrm{NH}}_{2}{\cdot}{\mathrm{MIL}}{\cdot}125({\mathrm{Ti}})$ belong to the symmetric and asymmetric stretching of amines explicating the triumphant synthesis(Figure 20b). Amination significantly strengthen the response of this sample in visible-light region and deepen the color from white to yellow (Figure 20c). The amount of captured $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ also increased from 98.6 to $132.2{\ c m}^{3}{\ \mathrm{g}}^{-1}$ . All of these enhance the amount of $\mathsf{H C O O^{-}}$ generated under irradiation to $8.14\ \upmu\mathrm{mol}$ in $^{10\mathrm{h}}$ over $\mathrm{NH}_{2}$ -MIL-125(Ti).
Similar to ${\mathrm{NH}}_{2}{\cdot}{\mathrm{MIL}}{\cdot}125({\mathrm{Ti}})$ , $\mathrm{NH}_{2^{-}}\mathrm{UiO}{-}66(\mathrm{Zr})$ has been fabricated to examine its photoreduction performance that reached $13.2~\upmu\mathrm{mol}$ ${\mathrm{HCOO^{-}}}$ in $^{10\mathrm{~h~}}$ higher than $\mathrm{NH}_{2}$ -MIL-125(Ti).[63] The authors attributed this result to that the redox potential of $\mathrm{Zr^{IV}/Z r^{I I I}}$ is more negative than $\mathrm{Ti^{IV}/T i^{I I I}}$ . The reaction mechanism has been studied by ESR spectra (Figure 21a). A signal with a $g$ value of 2.004 was appeared when the ligand (2-aminoterephthalate, ATA) was irradiated alone. However, a new ESR signal at $g=2.002$ belonged to ${\boldsymbol{Z}}{\mathrm{r}}^{\mathrm{III}}$ replaced the above signal under the circumstance that $\mathrm{NH}_{2^{-}}\mathrm{UiO}{-}66(\mathrm{Zr})$ was exposed to light. It indicates that photoinduced electrons will transfer from excited ligands to metal clusters to form active sites in catalytic process. In addition, the mixed $\mathrm{NH}_{2^{-}}\mathrm{UiO}{-}66(\mathrm{Zr})$ has been obtained for further exploration through partly substituting ATA by 2,5-diaminoterephthalic acid $\mathrm{(H}_{2}\mathrm{DTA)}$ . The increased the amount of amino enhances the light-harvesting ability and elevates the yield of $\operatorname{HCOO^{-}}$ (Figure 21b,c). So, amination is an intelligent approach to modify the organic linkers, though the more amino we incorporate, the more difficult we will meet.
Despite the improvement of photocatalytic activity, the modification on original ligands cannot change the frameworks thoroughly that limits its effect. So, it is necessary to design brand new linkers to constitute the structure of MOFs. Chen et al.[74] employed an anthracene group to obtain a carboxylate ligand $[\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{L},$ $^{4,4^{\prime}}$ -(anthracene-9,10-diylbis(ethyne-2,1-diyl))dibenzoic acid] which can harvest visible light and construct a new zirconium MOF (NNU-28). The analysis on X-ray single crystal diffraction shows that there are concave and convex cages in the lattices (Figure 22a) resulting from two extended alkynyl groups in $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{L}$ . As shown in Figure 22b,c, the visible-light absorption of NNU-28 is extremely intense and the amount of formate anions reaches $26.4~\upmu\mathrm{mol}$ under irradiation in $^{10\mathrm{~h~}}$ . The ligand also shows the photocatalytic ability producing $7.2\ \mathrm{\upmumol}$ $\scriptstyle{\mathrm{HCOO^{-}}}$ in $^{10\mathrm{~h~}}$ . Besides, surface photovoltage (SPV) spectroscopy (Figure 22d) has been monitored to provide information on the generation of excitons. Three main peaks at 436, 466, and $491~\mathrm{nm}$ of NUU-28 match well with those of the ligand suggesting the most photoinduced charges engender upon the excitation of the linkers. Further exploration on mechanism was inspired by these two phenomena. An ESR signal of $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{L}$ with a $g$ value of 2.003 was observed under illumination and it did not changed when $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{L}$ was substituted by NNU-28 (Figure 22e) indicating the radical over MOF form on the ligand. Nevertheless, two new signals at $g=2.009$ and 2.030 emerged under continuous light irradiation (Figure 22f) which are originated from the intervalence charge transport by the LMCT process meaning that the metal active sites appeard. In other words, NNU-28 possesses dual catalytic routes.
## 4.1.4. MOFs-Derived Catalysts
The photocatalytic performance of the materials combining MOFs and other compounds is originated from the interaction between metal clusters and ligands or just affected by it to some extent. However, this interaction is also the reason why the frameworks of MOFs cannot exist stably during the photocatalytic procedure. The calcination as one of the further treatment method on MOFs destroy this interaction, but the frameworks remain unchanged and inorganic metal or metal semiconductors such as oxides, nitrides, and sulfides will come into being. This strategy can handle the problem of material stability and preserve the catalytic ability. Certainly, it is necessary to construct a hybrid system to settle the problem of the fast electron–hole pair recombination.
To meet up with the requirement, Khaletskaya et  al.[167] have fabricated the gold/titania hybrid photocatalyst derived from ${\mathrm{NH}}_{2^{-}}{\mathrm{MIL}}{\cdot}125(\mathrm{Ti})$ . First, the MOF with gold nanoparticles (GNPs) was obtained by a solvothermal method heating the mixture of performed gold, aminoterephtalic acid $(\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{bdc}.\mathrm{NH}_{2})$ , and titanium isopropoxide. Second, the as-prepared $\mathrm{GNP}/\mathrm{NH}_{2}.$ MIL-125(Ti) was pyrolytic decomposed in $450~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ under $\mathrm{O}_{2}$ for $^{2\mathrm{~h~}}$ to attain $\mathrm{{GNP/TiO_{2}}}$ . The morphologies of these products are shown in Figure 23a,b. As the illustration, the shape does not change a lot after calcination. In order to evaluate the photocatalytic performance of this material, $\mathrm{TiO}_{2}$ derived from $\mathrm{NH}_{2}$ -MIL-125(Ti) was synthesized in the same condition and commercial P-25 as well as AUROlite $\left({\mathrm{Au}}/{\mathrm{TiO}_{2}}\right)$ ) was chosen to be the reference sample. As presented in Figure 23c, the most methane was produced over $\mathrm{{GNP/TiO_{2}}}$ and the reason has been investigated. It can be known from the XRD patterns of the pyrolytic samples (Figure 23d) that $\mathrm{{GNP/TiO}}_{2}$ possesses the typical rutile pattern while $\mathrm{TiO}_{2}$ also exhibits anatase reflections. It may be caused by the GNPs which can be the hot pot to increase the temperature on its sites. The rutile structure can suppress the recombination of photoinduced charges due to the less lattice defects which are known as the combination center. $\mathrm{N}_{2}$ sorption isotherms reveal that the BET surface area of $\mathrm{{GNP/TiO}}_{2}$ is $19.8~\mathrm{m}^{2}~\mathrm{g}^{-1}$ , which is really high as a kind of nonporous rutile. Additionally, $\mathrm{TiO}_{2}$ derived from MOFs may be doped by $\mathrm{~N~}$ or C atom which can tune its photochemical property. Certainly, the effect of noble metal as electron traps cannot be neglected.
Furthermore, researchers studied for the system of two metal oxides originated from MOFs, which also show synergistic effect on photocatalysis. $\mathrm{ZnO}@\mathrm{Co}_{3}\mathrm{O}_{4}$ hybrid material has been obtained and investigated on its catalytic performance.[168]
As shown in Figure 24a, ZIF-8 was achieved first and used as a seed to fabricate Z $\mathrm{IF}{\cdot}8@\mathrm{ZIF}{\cdot}67$ with the core–shell structure which is proved by the SEM images of ZIF-8 and ZIF-8 $@$ ZIF-67 (Figure 24b,c) where ZIF-8 $@$ ZIF-67 owns rougher surface. Then, through the two-step calcination, $\mathrm{ZnO}@\mathrm{Co}_{3}\mathrm{O}_{4}$ was synthesized successfully. $\mathrm{Co}_{3}\mathrm{O}_{4}$ nanoparticles are embedded in the $\mathsf{Z n O}$ surface depicted in the TEM image (Figure 24d). In addition, $z\mathrm{nO}$ derived from ZIF-8 was applied to be reference sample. This report focuses on the comparison between the catalysts before and after irradiation. The XRD patterns (Figure 24e,f) reveal that the structure of $z\mathrm{nO}$ was changed after reaction while that of $\mathrm{ZnO}@\mathrm{Co}_{3}\mathrm{O}_{4}$ remained. The XPS spectra (Figure 24g,h) also manifest that a new peak $(\approx289~\mathrm{eV})$ which is represent the existence of $\mathrm{CO}_{3}{}^{2-}$ appeared only on $z\mathrm{nO}$ after illumination and $\mathrm{CO}_{3}{}^{2-}$ is the outcome of the photocorrosion. Both results of the XRD and XPS indicate that ${\mathrm{Co}}_{3}{\mathrm{O}}_{4}$ can suppress the photocorrosion of $\mathrm{znO}$ and improve the stability of the system. Similar to $\mathrm{{GNP/TiO_{2}}}$ , the BET surface area of $\mathrm{ZnO}@\mathrm{Co}_{3}\mathrm{O}_{4}$ is about $25\mathrm{m}^{2}\mathrm{g}^{-1}$ . Although the value is much lower than that of the MOF precursor, it is really high among the metal oxides. Thus we can further select promising metal oxides system which is originated from MOFs for $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ reduction application.
Recently, MOF-derived porous composites have been developed as excellent photocatalysts. Lou and co-workers prepared $\mathrm{In}_{2}\mathrm{S}_{3}{\mathrm{-CdIn}_{2}\mathrm{S}_{4}}$ heterostructured hierarchical nanotubes for photocatalytic reduction of $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ (Figure 26).[170] They start with an In-based MOF (MIL-68), by utilizing solution infiltration method followed by heat treatment and cation exchange reaction, the as-prepared $\mathrm{In}_{2}\mathrm{S}_{3}$ tubular nanostructure is controllable transformed into a hierarchical $\mathrm{In}_{2}\mathrm{S}_{3}{\mathrm{-CdIn}_{2}\mathrm{S}_{4}}$ hybrid nanotubes.
They developed the new sequential ion exchange strategy to prepare hierarchical $\mathrm{In}_{2}\mathrm{S}_{3}{\mathrm{-CdIn}_{2}\mathrm{S}_{4}}$ heterostructured nanotubes. The optimized $\mathrm{In}_{2}\mathrm{S}_{3}{\mathrm{-CdIn}_{2}\mathrm{S}_{4}}$ nanotubes exhibited superior selectivity toward the photocatalytic reduction of $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ under visible-light irradiation, with a high CO production rate of $825~{\upmu\mathrm{mol}}~\mathrm{h}^{-\mathrm{\bar{1}}}~\mathrm{g}^{-1}$ , far exceeding that of $\mathrm{In}_{2}\mathrm{S}_{3}$ material. This observation highlights the remarkable $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ photoreduction performance which is associated with the unique heterostructure. The unique structure and composition facilitate the separation and mobility of electrons and holes, further more, large surface area $(68\mathrm{m}^{2}\mathrm{g}^{-1})$ promote the high concentration of $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ .
Later, they prepared another hollow architerctures with use of the In-MIL-68 as the precursor for $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ reduction. The asprepared $\mathrm{ZnIn_{2}S_{4}–I n_{2}O_{3}}$ shows double-heterojuncion shells and ultrathin 2D nanosheet subunits.[171] It exhibits outstanding catalyst property for reductive $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ deoxygenation with considerable CO generation rate $(3075~\mathrm{\upmumol~h^{-1}~g^{-1}})$ and high stability. They further proved the importance of MOF-derived porous composites for $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ reduction.
Including structural defects into MOFs is also the key for promoting catalysis. Niu et al. reported a spongy nickel–organic heterogeneous photocatalyst via introduce a high concentration of defects.[172] The catalyst has a crystalline network architecture with a high concentration of defects. It is highly active in converting $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ to CO, with a production rate of ${\approx}1.6\times10^{4}$ mmol $\mathrm{{h^{-1}\ g^{-1}}}$ . It is nearly $100\%$ selective CO production over $\mathrm{H}_{2}$ evolution. If enrich spongy Ni–organic catalyst with Rh or Ag nanocrystals, the controlled photocatalytic $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ reduction reactions will generate formic acid and acetic acid.[172] This study enriched the $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ reduction study on defected MOFs.
In order to get efficient MOF catalyst for $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ reduction, new method also needed to create dynamic gas/liquid interfaces to maximize the contact of $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ , $\mathrm{H}_{2}$ , and $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}$ . Lin and co-workers designed an MOF catalyst with use a Soxhlet-type reflux-condensing system (Figure 27).[173] And the catalyst achieve a high turnover frequency of $410\mathrm{h}^{-1}$ under atmospheric pressure and at $85~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ . This work provide a opportunities to use MOFs as solid molecular catalysts in designer reactors to enhance the catalysis efficiency.[173]
## 4.2. Electrocatalytic Reduction of $\mathsf{c o}_{2}$
$\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ electroreduction is a very complicated process due to the huge amount of reduction products. A large overpotential is used to obtain a substantial yield, the formation of which has a high activation energy barrier. The reaction mechanism and products vary depending on electrode/catalyst materials and reaction medium.
Faraday efficiency of $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ reduction is affected by the competing hydrogen evolution while CO and HCOOH were both obtained as the major reduction products. 32 metals were divided into four groups according to the major reduction products: HCOOH, CO, $\mathrm{H}_{2}$ , and hydrocarbon.[174] Due to the complex situation, design the catalysts with high selectivity becomes the key challenge. The challenge in catalyst design is to select an acid- and water-stable catalyst that can operate with a low overpotential to reduce the undesirable hydrogen by-products. MOFs can serve as a promising platform for achieving high electrocatalytic performances and studying the electrocatalytic mechanisms.[174] Here we simply conclude the reported results of MOF-based electrocatalyst reduction of $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ . Among the several common transition metals, Cu is highly recommended for $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ reduction electrocatalyst among the transition metals. Because Cu is the only one with considerable production of hydrocarbons while still retaining a relatively high overall carbon reduction efficiency, and Cu-based electrodes were widely researched particularly.[175] MOFs for electrocatalytic reduction of $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ are summarized in Table 4.
In 2012, Yamada and co-workers synthesized a copper rubeanate MOF which can electrochemically reduce $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ to formic acid, this is the first Cu-based MOF in the electrochemical reduction of $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ This Cu-based MOF shows an onset potential of $0.2\mathrm{~V~}$ which is more positive than that of the benchmarking Cu metal electrode. In this MOF structure, surrounding of the metal reaction sites in MOF materials has very limited space due to the presence of nanopores, the high selectivity was attributed to the relatively weak adsorption of $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ to the ionic $\mathrm{Cu}^{2+}$ site in the MOF when compared with the adsorption strength of $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ to metallic $\mathtt{C u}$ (Figure 28a,b). Despite the high selectivity for the reduction of $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ a Faradaic efficiency of only $30\%$ was achieved, with a large amount of $\mathrm{H}_{2}$ by-product.[140] The selectivity of HCOOH among the $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ reduction products was more than $98\%$ .
In the same year, another Cu-based MOF was studied for electrochemical reduction of $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ by Kumar and co-workers (Figure 29a–c). They fabricated electrocatalytic $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ reduction with $\mathrm{Cu}_{3}(\mathrm{BTC})_{2}$ (also denoted as HKUST-1) which was performed with open metal sites. The use of nonaqueous solvent (DMF) led to the production of oxalic acid with $90\%$ selectivity, which was proposed to occur by dimerization of $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ (Figure 29d). Similar to the previous example, the Faradaic efficiency was relatively low $(51\%)$ and the mechanism was attributed to the reduction of $\mathrm{{Cu(II)}}$ to $\mathtt{C u(I)}$ followed by electron transfer to the bound $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ . This $\mathtt{C u(I)}$ forms adduct dioxide and electrochemically forms oxalic acid, which is confirmed through bulk electrolysis and analysis using GCMS.[84]
In order to construct the more efficiency MOF-based electrochemical $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ reduction system, in 2015, Yang and co-workers[96] selected MOFs with catalytic linker units and fabricated them into thin films covering conductive substrates, Co–porphyrin MOF (Figure 30a,b). Porphyrin-based molecular units previo­ usly reported as selective and efficient homogeneous $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ - reduction electrocatalysts. The prepared $\mathrm{Al}_{2}(\mathrm{OH})_{2}\mathrm{TCPP-C}$ o, which has been demonstrated as catalysts for electroreduction of $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ to CO with CO selectivity in excess of $76\%$ in aqueous electrolytes (Figure 30c), and a high TON of 1400 for the stable catalyst. In addition to $\mathrm{Co-N}$ sites, the porphyrin linkers played important role in $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ reduction by acting as electrocatalytic centers and redox-hopping-based conduits for charge transfer. The robust structure provides both large arrays of Co–N sites and simultaneous reduction properties of porphyrin linkers. This study represents the development of $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ reduction electrocatalysts in which the active sites, inorganic backbone, and thickness/loading were designed and resulting MOF integrated onto a conductive support.[96] The modularity of these systems yields many opportunities to further improve the performance and open new directions in electrocatalysis.
Furthermore, Hod et al.,[80] as a proof of principle, a metal– porphyrin based MOF $\mathrm{[Zr_{6}O_{4}(O H)_{4}(F e(t c p p))_{3}C l_{3}]}$ (Fe-MOF-525)
was selected to compare with corresponding homogeneous Fe– porphyrin catalysts in DMF. MOF-525 is a 3D MOF with high chemical stability constructed from 4-bridged $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{tcpp}^{2-}$ , free porphyrin-based ligands, and 12-connected $\mathrm{Zr}_{6}\mathrm{O}_{4}(\mathrm{OH})_{4}(\mathrm{RCOO})_{12}$ clusters. MOF-525 microcrystals $(300-500\mathrm{nm})$ ) were electrophoretically deposited onto the fluorine-doped tin oxide (FTO) as nanosized thin film, then treated by post synthetic method with $\mathrm{Fe}^{3+}$ to give the work electrode: Fe-MOF-525 (Figure 31a–c). At $E=-1.3\mathrm{~V~}$ versus SHE in the $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ -saturated $1\mathrm{~M~}$ tetrabutylammonium hexafluorophosphate acetonitrile electrolyte, the Fe-MOF-525 electrode yielded CO ( $\mathrm{FE}=54\%$ , TOF $64~\mathrm{{h^{-1}}}$ 1 and $\mathrm{H}_{2}$ $\mathrm{FE}=45\%$ ) (Figure 31d). The chemical products of the reduction, obtained with $\approx100\%$ Faradaic efficiency, are mixtures of CO and $\mathrm{H}_{2}$ .
The exhibited high electrocatalytic activity for $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ reduction, is due to the high areal concentration of the active Fe-TPP on the electrode surface as well as the regular nanoporosity. This work represents a significant step forward in the heterogenization of molecular electrocatalysts for energy-relevant reactions under high-flux conditions.
Besides metal–porphyrin, Han and co-workers[83] deposited sheet-like Zn-MOFs ( $_{[Z\mathrm{n-1},3,5}$ -benzenetricarboxylate) onto the carbon papers, they combined Zn-MOF/CP cathodes and ionic liquid electrolytes for electrochemical reduction of $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ to $\mathrm{CH}_{4}$ . The morphology of the Zn-MOFs has significant effect on the electrochemical reaction. The sheetlike $Z\mathrm{n}$ -MOF has the highest activity in the $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ reduction due to its largest electroactive surface areas, and the imidazolium-based ionic liquids (ILs) with fluorine are more effective electrolytes because fluorine has stronger interaction with $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ (Figure 32a). The Zn-MOF-based cathodes and the ILs are excellent combination for selective and efficient electrochemical $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ reduction to $\mathrm{CH}_{4}$ in the imidazolium-based ILs electrolytes. The sheet-like morphology of the $Z\mathrm{n}$ -MOFs has significant effect on the electrochemical reaction, it shows the highest activity in the $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ reduction due to its largest electroactive surface areas. It was the first report on the combination of an MOF electrode and ionic liquid electrolyte in the electrochemical reduction of $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ . The imidazolium-based ILs with fluorine are more effective electrolytes due to the stronger interaction between ILs with $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ . This $Z\mathrm{n}$ -MOF-based cathodes and the ILs are excellent combination for the efficient and selective reduction of $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ to $\mathrm{CH}_{4}$ . The selectivity of $\mathrm{CH}_{4}$ can be higher than $80\%$ .[83]
Based on the above results, thin film morphology provides large electroactive surface areas, researchers are mainly preparing film-like morphology to enhance the $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ reduction. Later Sun and co-workers[82] prepared a monolithic, highly oriented Re-complexes incorporated MOF thin films, $\mathrm{ReL}(\mathrm{CO})_{3}\mathrm{Cl}$ $[\mathrm{L}=2,2^{\prime}$ -bipyridine- $.5,5^{\prime}.$ dicarboxylic acid), also denoted as ReSURMOF, after deposited it onto conductive FTO substrates, which can selectively and efficiently reduce $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ to CO with high Faradaic efficiency of ${\approx}93\%$ for CO production (Figure 32b). They also combined DFT calculations to further confirm the 2D sheet-like structure of the FTO electrode. The Re-SURMOFbased electrodes show high efficiency as well as high selectivity for the reduction of $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ to CO over Re-linker-based molecular catalysts. However, the stability of this Re-based MOF catalysts needs to be further improved.[82]
Cu-based MOFs has been used as electrocatalysts for $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ reduction, Albo showed that HKUST-1 and $\mathrm{[Cu_{2}(a d e)_{2}(C H_{3}C O O)_{2}]}$ (denoted as CuAdeAce) can also act as electrocatalysts for $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ reduction to methanol and ethanol.[177] CuAdeAce is a 3D framework, with the the $\mathtt{C u(I I)}$ ions coordinated in a square pyramidal fashion by three nitrogen atoms from two ade− ligands and two carboxylic ligands, without uncoordinated sites and terminal coordinated solvent molecules (Figure 33a). CuAdeAce exhibits poor catalytic activity due to its lack of guest accessible site. The HKUST-1 and CuAdeAce electrodes showed stable electrocatalytic performance for 17 and $^{12\mathrm{~h~}}$ , respectively. However, significant reductions in crystallinity were observed for both MOFs, indicating the degradation of the catalysts (Figure 33b).[177]
Besides above Cu-based MOFs and porphyrin-supported MOFs, three additional copper-complexes were studied for electrocatalytic $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ reduction. The copper(II) phthalocyanine exhibits the highest activity for yielding methane to the best of our knowledge,[178] with a Faradaic efficiency of $66\%$ and a partial current density of $13\mathrm{\mA\cm^{-2}}$ at the potential of $-1.06\mathrm{~V~}$ versus the reversible hydrogen electrode. CuPc exhibits a different restructuring behavior and thus different catalytic properties from HKUST-1 and $\mathrm{[Cu(cyclam)lCl_{2}}$ . CuPc reversibly forms $\mathrm{{Cu}}$ nanoclusters under the reaction conditions. Restructuring of $\mathrm{{Cu}}$ complexes in electrocatalytic materials for $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ reduction to $\mathrm{CH}_{4}$ has been elucidated by in situ and operando XAS characterization of representative Cu complex structures.
Recently, researchers prepared stable N-doped porous carbon by pyrolysis of multiwalled carbon nanotubes (MWCNTs)- supported ZIF-8 for electrochemical $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ reduction. They used a hybrid material composed of pyrolyzed ZIFs and MWCNTs to selectively catalyze $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ to CO, they performed the catalytic reaction in aqueous solution with close to $100\%$ Faradaic efficiency and a current density up to $7.7\ \mathrm{mA}\ \mathrm{cm}^{-2}$ at an over potential of $740~\mathrm{mV.^{[179]}}$ This work highlights the importance of optimizing electron and mass transport in turning moderate catalysts into superior ones with high selectivity and activity for $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ reduction. Their work provides new insights into designing N-doped and M-N-doped porous carbon materials for $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ reduction.
## 5. Conclusions and Outlooks
In this progress report, we have presented an overview of progress in MOF-based materials for $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ reduction applications. We summarized the recent development of MOFs for $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ reduction, including electrocatalysis and photocatalysis. All these examples provided in this review give a deeper understanding in preparing highly active and stable MOFbased $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ reduction materials. MOF-based materials consistently show high product selectivity due to the catalytic sites and restricted reaction spaces.
Low efficiency for charge separation and energy transfer, as well as inconsistent between catalytic active and adsorption sites are the main drawbacks for photocatalytic $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ reduction. For electrocatalysis, limited research had been reported about electrocatalytic $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ reduction with using MOFs, the mainly drawback is their poor electrical conductivity and limited mass permeability of MOFs, as the electron transfer from the catalytic active sites highly relies on their intimate and stable bind interaction with $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ . Low reduction efficiency resulted by a mixture of products, making the process expensive by increasing the cost of product separation. Thus investigations for $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ reduction using MOFs-based materials are still in highly demand. Enhancing $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ uptake capacity is an essential point to address for promoting the $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ reaction efficiency. MOF-based materials are unique compared with other materials used for $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ reduction, which is due to their high designability, high adsorption capacity for $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ , uniform distribution of catalytic sites and confined reaction spaces. Careful selection of the MOF is necessary to design a new MOFbased catalyst for $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ reduction, the metal centers should have a lower redox potential relative to the lowest unoccupied molecular orbital of the organic ligand for accepting the photogenerated electrons. While the organic ligands should be selected from which are responsible for absorbing light to generate excited electrons. In the meanwhile, with optimized morphologies/sizes, MOF-based materials can give high catalytic activities. Increasing the electric conductivities of MOF crystals and the substances can be very promising for improving the electrocatalysis performances.
MOFs offer great opportunities for designing MOF-based materials for $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ reduction. In the future, the development strategies of MOFs for $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ reduction are to enhance their thermal, mechanical, and chemical stability. Incorporate more functional groups, precious metal nanoparticles, and precious/ nonprecious metal complexes into MOFs, will offer great potential to provide exciting opportunities for numbers of $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ reduction applications in the future (Scheme 1).
The material with $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ reduction and simultaneously incorporating conductivity, magnetic property, fluorescence function is still a significant challenge. Creation of MOF-based materials with the multifunctionality in the future could provide new pathways to functional MOF-based materials, alongside opportunities for open up a new area with MOFs not only fundamentally but also for industrial applications.
